  "narrative": "All future estimates remain already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 main-effect claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a more negative dependency score and therefore stronger dependency. For direction +1 main-effect claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a less negative score and therefore weaker dependency. For interaction claims, a positive signed estimate means the signed main effect is larger in the focal subgroup than in the complement. These are dataset associations in the supplied lung CRISPR dataset, not causal or clinically generalizable claims.\n\nThe accepted validated effects are now well established: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency. The next useful questions are not more confirmation of those main effects, but specificity, context modification, and orthogonal predictors. The first proposal asks whether the WRN association is driven by MSI-high-specific mismatch-repair biology rather than generic high mutation burden. By interacting MSI-high with high mutation burden, we ask whether the WRN dependency contrast is larger among high-mutation-burden lines than among lower-mutation-burden lines. A large positive interaction would suggest a mutation-burden/MSI continuum; a near-null or negative interaction would support MSI-specific biology beyond generic TMB.\n\nThe second proposal tests whether the accepted PTEN loss STAT3 effect is serum/context dependent. PTEN loss alters PI3K-AKT signaling and STAT3 is a signaling-responsive survival node; serum growth factors could amplify or buffer that dependency. I register the accepted direction, direction -1, and use an uncertain interaction expectation because the biology could go either way: high serum may increase growth-factor-driven STAT3 dependence, or it may reduce the relative importance of PTEN loss. A strong positive interaction would indicate context dependence; a null interaction would support robustness across serum levels.\n\nThe third proposal tests whether SMARCA4 loss synthetic lethality with SMARCA2 is modified by low 9p21 copy number, a proxy for CDKN2A-region loss. This is a focused chromatin and cell-cycle stress question. If SMARCA2 dependency depends on broader SWI/SNF, cell-cycle, or proteotoxic stress states, low 9p21 may amplify it. If SMARCA2 dependency is highly specific to SMARCA4 loss, the interaction should be near null. The accepted direction is retained, so a positive signed interaction would mean the SMARCA4 effect is larger in low-9p21 lines.\n\nThe fourth proposal tests whether the accepted KEAP1 loss effect on NFE2L2 is modified by high research_signature_a. The signature has arbitrary units and no assigned biology, so this is primarily an orthogonal assay-robustness screen. If the KEAP1-NFE2L2 association is strong but confined to particular expression contexts, the interaction may be nonzero; if it is a dominant genomic marker effect, the interaction should remain small. Because the accepted NFE2L2 effect is direction +1, the interaction direction +1 means a larger weaker-dependency effect in high-signature lines.\n\nThe fifth proposal introduces NF1 loss as a new predictor for BRAF dependency. The mechanistic hypothesis is that NF1 is a RAS GTPase-activating protein, so NF1 loss can activate RAS downstream of BRAF and thereby reduce BRAF dependency. The claim direction is +1 for weaker BRAF dependency, and the anticipated raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast is positive. Plausible alternatives are no effect or even co-selection for BRAF dependency; a negative or small estimate would argue against bypass logic in this dataset.\n\nThe sixth proposal tests MTAP loss against stronger POLR2A dependency. MTAP loss is an untested measured genetic lesion, often relevant to metabolic and epigenetic stress states. If such lesions increase replication or transcription-associated stress, dependence on the core RNA polymerase II machinery could increase. The hypothesis is specific: MTAP loss is associated with stronger POLR2A dependency, direction -1, expected raw negative exposed-minus-comparator contrast. A near-null or opposite estimate would close this candidate unless another mechanistic route warrants refinement.\n\nThe final three proposals use the constructed binary research markers as orthogonal predictors for dependency_SMARCA2, dependency_WRN, and dependency_STAT3. These markers have no assigned gene, pathway, or clinical role, so they should be interpreted descriptively rather than mechanistically. The registered direction -1 tests the hypothesis that marker positivity is associated with stronger dependency. The expectation is uncertain because the markers are constructed assays. If one produces a large positive signed effect with an interval excluding zero, it would become a candidate descriptive predictor worth validation; if it is near null, that is expected and will be closed without implying biological irrelevance beyond this dataset. Overall, these proposals allocate analysis toward specificity tests for the strongest accepted effects, targeted known-pathway hypotheses not yet tested, and broad orthogonal marker/signature screens."
